For random \textsc{$k$-SAT}, one considers formulas with $N$ Boolean variables and $M$ clauses. The natural order parameter is then the \emph{clause density}
\begin{align}
    \alpha = \frac{M}{N},
\end{align}
which fixes the number of constraints per variable. 
For fixed $N$, many random formulas are generated at a given $\alpha$, and the satisfiability probability
\begin{align}
